      "category": "drivetrain",
      "title": "Cracking differential mount (studs rip out of the frame)",
      "description": "The diff is hung from the frame on a forward 'mustache' bracket and rear mounts. The forward differential mounting points are prone to cracking; the right-front point fails first because it carries the most torque load. As the mount works loose the crack propagates and, if ignored, the mounting studs eventually tear out of the frame, letting the diff move under power. The classic tell is a rear-end 'clunk' on sudden throttle application that starts intermittent and progresses to every power-on/off transition.",
      "solution": "If caught early you may only need fresh rubber diff mounting bushings, but the proven bulletproofing fix is a reinforcement kit: weld in a Moss/Sport Car Parts differential-mount repair kit that adds new studs, brackets and (on the front) reinforcing plates that spread the load and stop the crack from returning. The job is done with the diff out — grind out and weld up any existing crack, fit the reinforcing plate over the cross-member, install the new pin/studs, and renew the mount bushings at the same time. Polyurethane diff mount bushings are a common durability upgrade over the soft OEM rubber.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Rear 'clunk' on sudden throttle on/off",
        "Driveline shudder or movement when loading/unloading the drivetrain",
        "Visible crack at the forward diff mount cross-member",
        "Loose or pulled diff mounting studs",
        "Worn/collapsed rubber diff mount bushings"
      ],
