      "category": "engine",
      "title": "Crankshaft thrust-washer wear and excessive end float",
      "description": "The straight-six uses semicircular (half-moon) crankshaft thrust washers at the centre main rather than full-circle thrusts, and the soft lead-indium face wears quickly. As they wear, crankshaft end float grows beyond the 0.006–0.008 in spec; past about 0.015 in the coating is gone and once the alloy wears through to steel it accelerates, scoring the crank thrust faces and eventually letting a washer drop into the sump. Riding the clutch at stoplights drives the wear hard. Worst case the crank walks fore-and-aft and destroys the block thrust faces — a full bottom-end job.",
      "solution": "Check end float with a dial indicator (lever the crank fore/aft) at every opportunity and especially after break-in; if it exceeds ~0.010–0.012 in, pull the centre main cap and fit oversize thrust washers to bring float back to 0.006–0.008 in. Restorers bulletproof this during a rebuild by fitting quality thrust washers and, on the best builds, having the bearing holder machined for bronze thrust washers/bushings instead of the original whitemetal. The cheapest insurance is the driving habit the community insists on: put the gearbox in neutral and release the clutch at stoplights instead of sitting with the pedal down.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Measured end float over 0.008 in growing toward 0.015 in",
        "Knock or fore-aft crank movement felt at the clutch",
        "Metallic flakes/half-moon washer found in the sump",
        "Crankshaft thrust face scoring on teardown",
        "Low oil pressure / debris after break-in of a fresh rebuild"
      ],
