      ],
      "category": "electrical",
      "title": "Lucas charging and electrical gremlins (corroded earths, weak alternator)",
      "description": "Like most British cars of the era the TR6 runs Lucas electrics with bullet-connector harnesses, a 16/17ACR alternator and lots of in-line earths through the body and bulb holders. Decades of corrosion at terminal blocks, the positive battery cable connections, the brown feed wires and the ignition-warning-light circuit (alternator brown/yellow + ignition white) cause flickering lights, no-charge faults, and a warning lamp that lies. Owners report that even a brand-new harness won't fix a charging fault when the real problem is a corroded earth or a tired regulator/alternator.",
      "solution": "Restorers cure it at the root: fit a quality reproduction wiring harness, then clean and protect every earth point (engine-to-frame and frame-to-body bonding straps added), and clean the brown feed wires and battery terminals. The popular bulletproofing upgrades are converting the points distributor to electronic ignition (Pertronix), and swapping the old 16ACR-style alternator for a modern higher-output internally-regulated alternator so the gauges and any added electric fan have reliable current. Note: with electronic ignition, never crank or run with a plug lead off — it can fry the module.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Ignition/no-charge warning light glowing or flickering",
        "Battery not charging despite a 'good' alternator test",
        "Dim or intermittent lights and gauges",
        "Voltage that drops with revs / wandering ammeter",
        "Hard starting traced to corroded brown feed or earth connections"
      ],
