      "category": "fuel",
      "title": "Lucas PI petrol-injection: hot-start failure, blocked injectors, fuel surge",
      "description": "European-market TR6 PI cars use the Lucas mechanical petrol-injection system, fed by a Lucas high-pressure pump. It is notoriously difficult to set up and live with: hard hot-starting after standing, blocked injectors, throttle-shaft wear that wrecks the mixture balance, and a pump/metering unit that ages poorly. Early TR5/TR6 PI tanks also lack an anti-surge reservoir, so on a tight left-hander below a quarter tank the engine fuel-starves and stumbles. This is the single most temperamental system on the car for a restorer.",
      "solution": "Two proven paths. Keep-it-original camp: rebuild the metering unit and injectors, replace worn throttle shafts, fit a modern Bosch high-pressure pump conversion kit (Revington TR) in place of the failure-prone original Lucas pump, and install a tank with the correct anti-surge reservoir to cure cornering starvation. Reliability-first camp: convert to twin Stromberg/SU or Weber carburettors (the US-spec setup) for far easier tuning and dependable starting, accepting a small power trade. Either way, address the anti-surge tank so the car doesn't cut out in corners.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Won't restart when hot after standing",
        "Rough idle and uneven full-throttle from throttle-shaft wear",
        "Stumble/cut-out on left turns with under a quarter tank",
        "Blocked injector causing a dead/weak cylinder",
        "Failing Lucas high-pressure pump (noise, low pressure)"
      ],
