      "category": "cooling",
      "title": "Marginal cooling / overheating in traffic and hot weather",
      "description": "The TR6's small 16 x 16.5 in radiator and engine-driven fan leave little cooling margin, so cars run hot idling in traffic and in hot climates, especially once the original brass core is partially silted up or the timing/mixture isn't right. The factory fan moves little air at idle, and a clogged or tired radiator pushes temperatures into the danger zone in stop-and-go driving.",
      "solution": "Bulletproof the system as a package rather than chasing one part: refresh the basics first (verify the thermostat opens, correct belt tension, healthy water pump, full flush) then fit an aluminum cross-flow/high-capacity radiator (Wizard Cooling, US Radiator) sized for the TR6, and add a thermostatically-switched electric cooling fan (e.g. Hayden/Spal ~14 in) which the community reports drops idle temps roughly 6–10°F and removes the parasitic drag of the engine fan. A 13-blade 'tropical' fan is the cheaper interim fix if staying engine-driven. Always confirm ignition timing and mixture, since a lean/retarded TR6 runs hot.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Temperature climbing while idling or in slow traffic",
        "Coolant loss / boiling over in hot weather",
        "Runs cool on the move but heats up when stopped",
        "Old/silted brass radiator core",
        "Engine-driven fan moving little air at idle"
      ],
