      "category": "suspension",
      "title": "Perished rear-suspension bushings causing rear camber/handling vagueness",
      "description": "The TR6's semi-trailing-arm independent rear suspension relies on soft rubber bushings at the trailing-arm pivots and fixed (non-adjustable) factory brackets. After decades the rubber perishes and collapses, letting rear camber and toe wander under load, which gives the car a vague, darty rear end, uneven inner-edge tyre wear, and clunks. The fixed factory bracket also can't correct the excessive negative camber a sagged car develops, and stresses the inner mount (a contributor to the trailing-arm bracket cracking restorers see).",
      "solution": "The standard bulletproofing rebuild is polyurethane trailing-arm bushings (SuperPro/Prothane) in place of the worn rubber for a firmly-planted rear without a harsh ride, combined with Goodparts/Richard Good adjustable trailing-arm brackets so rear camber can actually be set correctly instead of living with the factory's excess negative camber. Do it alongside fresh shocks/springs and a full inspection of the trailing-arm mounts, since the same area is the chassis crack/rust hot-spot. Properly set up, owners report flat, predictable cornering and even tyre wear.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Vague or darty rear end under power and over bumps",
        "Excessive negative rear camber / inner-edge tyre wear",
        "Clunks from the rear trailing-arm pivots",
        "Collapsed or cracked rubber trailing-arm bushings",
        "Rear toe/camber that changes as the car is loaded"
      ],
